# A null Array descriptor reference used by an executed operation -- `length`, an element read, an element write -- is the
# ordinary implicit `interpreter` throw in both modes, taken by `catch: interpreter ()` (Codex, CODEX-NULL-ARRAY-20261011-140439;
# the book, exceptions; steps/defects.md NULL-ARRAY-REFERENCE-READ-DIVERGES).  Before: natively "lmx: invariant: a field path
# met no Structure", abort (exit 3).  g binds the empty cell a[0] (no row stored).  The control: a zero-length Array is a real
# descriptor -- `length` of a binding to an uncounted nested own Array is 0 and nothing is thrown.  Entry 7.
fn: len_null () int
    []: []: int a 2
    []: g: a[0]
    size_t: n 0U
    int: got 1
    ---
        n: length(g)
        got: 2
    ---
    catch: interpreter ()
        got: 7
return: got

fn: read_null () int
    []: []: int a 2
    []: g: a[0]
    int: got 1
    ---
        got: g[0]
    ---
    catch: interpreter ()
        got: 7
return: got

fn: write_null () int
    []: []: int a 2
    []: g: a[0]
    int: got 1
    ---
        g[0]: 5
        got: 2
    ---
    catch: interpreter ()
        got: 7
return: got

fn: zero_length () int
    int: []: []: z
    []: []: zb z
    if: length(zb) != 0U
        return: 81
    return: 7
end: zero_length

fn: work () int
    if: len_null() != 7
        return: 82
    if: read_null() != 7
        return: 83
    if: write_null() != 7
        return: 84
    return: zero_length()
end: work
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: work(); stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
